\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Script 9 You Already Enter a Hypnotic State Every Single Day\
Here is something that might change how you think about hypnosis entirely.\
You have already been in a hypnotic state today. Probably more than once.\
Every single day, without any therapist, without any technique, without any intention at all, your brain naturally drifts through the alpha and theta states that hypnosis uses deliberately.\
Every morning when you wake up, before you fully come to consciousness, your brain is in theta. Slow. Drifting. Deeply relaxed. Open.\
Every night when you fall asleep, your brain passes through alpha and then theta on its way down to sleep.\
And throughout the day, any time you zone out, get lost in thought, find yourself staring out of a window with no idea how much time has passed, or feel that calm quiet drift while listening to music or taking a long drive on a familiar road, your brain has slipped into alpha.\
You already do this. Naturally. Automatically. Without trying.\
What hypnotherapy does is take that same natural ability and make it intentional. It gives you a way to enter that state on purpose, stay in it for long enough to do meaningful work, and direct it toward something specific you want to change.\
You are not learning something new. You are learning to use something you already have. Follow for more.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Script 10 That Feeling Before You Fall Asleep Is More Powerful Than You Think\
You know that feeling. The day is done. You are lying in bed. The thoughts start to slow down. The body gets heavy. The edges of everything go soft.\
That is not nothing. That is one of the most significant brain states you ever enter.\
In those moments between full wakefulness and sleep, your brain has shifted out of beta and is moving through alpha into theta. The critical analytical conscious mind is stepping back. And the deeper layers of the mind are becoming available.\
This is the hypnagogic state. And it is remarkable.\
In this state the brain is extraordinarily open to suggestion, to imagery, to emotion, to new associations. The filters that protect you from taking things too seriously in waking life are relaxed. What you expose your mind to in this state lands differently. More deeply. More lastingly.\
This is why what you think about just before sleep matters so much. The worries you rehearse. The resentments you replay. The fears you revisit. Your brain is in a state of deep receptivity and it absorbs all of it.\
But it also absorbs the good. The intentions you set. The version of yourself you imagine. The things you choose to feel grateful for. In this state those land deeply too.\
You have a window every single night. The question is what you choose to put through it. Follow for more.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Script 11 That Groggy Sunday Morning Feeling Is Actually a Superpower\
You know those slow Sunday mornings. No alarm. No urgency. You are half awake, half asleep, drifting somewhere in between. Thoughts come and go without much effort. Time feels elastic. The world feels far away.\
Most people just wait for that feeling to pass so they can get on with the day.\
But that state is one of the most valuable mental states you can be in.\
What you are experiencing is your brain in theta. The same state used in hypnotherapy. The same state associated with deep creativity, emotional processing, and genuine openness to change.\
In that drifting morning state, your analytical mind has not fully come back online yet. The part of you that judges and evaluates and second guesses is still slow and quiet. Which means the deeper parts of your mind are unusually accessible.\
Artists and writers have known this for centuries. Many of the most creative insights in history have come in that half awake morning state. Your brain is doing something genuinely extraordinary in those moments. Making connections it cannot make when the analytical mind is running at full speed.\
Instead of rushing through it next time, try staying in it a little longer. Notice what comes up. Notice what your mind drifts toward when nothing is demanding its attention.\
You might be surprised what is in there. Follow for more.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Script 12 What the Gateway Into Trance Actually Is\
People imagine going into trance as something dramatic. A sudden shift. A click. A moment where you cross a threshold and everything changes.\
It is actually much quieter than that. And much more familiar.\
The gateway into trance is alpha. That calm, relaxed, eyes closed state that your brain drifts into naturally when you slow down. When you breathe deeply. When you let the noise of the day start to fade.\
Alpha sits right between your normal busy waking state and the deeper theta state where transformative work happens. It is the bridge. And crossing it does not feel like going somewhere strange. It feels like coming home.\
In alpha, the body relaxes. The breathing slows. The inner critic gets quieter. The sense of urgency and pressure that drives normal waking life begins to dissolve. Not because you have gone unconscious but because you have stopped fighting your own nervous system for a moment.\
From alpha, theta is a natural continuation. A deepening. The mind becomes more inward focused. More receptive. More open to the kind of deep change that thinking and willpower alone cannot reach.\
The interesting thing is that most people have been dismissing this gateway their entire lives. Treating it as just the space between being awake and being asleep. Not realizing it is one of the most powerful places their mind ever goes.\
You do not have to force your way into trance. You just have to stop forcing your way out of it. Follow for more.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Script 13 Hypnotherapy Does Not Create Something New It Makes Something Deliberate\
There is a common assumption about hypnotherapy that stops a lot of people from trying it.\
The assumption is that it introduces something foreign into your mind. That a hypnotherapist reaches in and rewires you in ways you cannot access yourself. That it is something done to you rather than something that happens through you.\
That is not what hypnotherapy is.\
Every state that hypnotherapy uses is a state you already enter naturally. Every night before sleep. Every morning before you fully wake. Every time you get lost in thought or zone out on a long drive or feel that calm dreamy drift on a slow afternoon.\
Your brain already knows how to do this. It has been doing it your whole life.\
What hypnotherapy does is take that natural ability and make it intentional. It creates a structured, purposeful entry into the alpha and theta states. It keeps you there long enough to do meaningful work. And it directs that receptive, open state toward the specific beliefs, patterns and responses you want to change.\
The difference between what your brain does naturally and what happens in hypnotherapy is not the state itself. It is the intention behind entering it.\
You already have the hardware. Hypnotherapy just teaches you how to use it on purpose. Follow for more.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Script 14 The Difference Between Alpha and Theta and Why Both Matter\
Alpha and theta are neighbors. But they are not the same place. And understanding the difference matters more than most people realize.\
Alpha runs at 8 to 12 cycles per second. It is the state of calm relaxed awareness. You are conscious, present and aware but the busy analytical chatter of normal waking life has quieted down. This is the gateway state. The bridge. The place where your mind begins to open.\
Theta runs slower, at 4 to 8 cycles per second. This is deeper. More inward. More removed from external awareness. In theta, the conscious critical mind has stepped significantly further back. The deeper layers of the mind, the ones that hold your beliefs, your memories, your emotional imprints, your ingrained patterns, are now genuinely accessible.\
Alpha is where you begin to relax into openness. Theta is where the real work happens.\
Think of it this way. Alpha is standing in the doorway. Theta is being fully inside the room.\
In alpha you can start to see things differently. In theta you can actually change them.\
Most forms of meditation work primarily in alpha. Deep hypnotherapy works in theta. Both are valuable. Both are natural. Both are states your brain already knows how to reach.\
The more you understand where you are in the spectrum, the more deliberately you can use each state for what it does best. Follow for more.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Script 15 Why Your Brain Is More Open to Change in Certain States\
Have you ever noticed that some moments just feel different? More open. More clear. More like real change might actually be possible.\
That is not random. That is your brain state.\
In normal waking beta consciousness, your brain is analytical, critical and evaluative. It is constantly assessing, judging, and protecting its existing model of how things are and who you are. This is useful for navigating daily life. But it is also what makes change so hard.\
Every time you try to install a new belief or a new behavior in beta, the critical mind is right there to challenge it. You cannot just decide to believe something new when the analytical brain is running at full speed. It will fact check you against everything you have ever experienced. And it will usually win.\
But in alpha and theta, the critical faculty relaxes. The gatekeeping quiets down. The part of your mind that insists on keeping things as they are loosens its grip.\
And in that loosening, new information can land differently. New beliefs can take root. New associations can form between things that were previously locked in old painful patterns.\
This is not about bypassing your intelligence or your judgment. It is about accessing a part of your mind that your intelligence and judgment normally drown out.\
The brain is not the same in every state. And knowing which state to be in for which kind of work might be the most useful thing you ever learn about yourself. Follow for more.\
